---
title: Helper Text
category: Components
description: Provides additional context or guidance for form inputs, like hints or error messages, supporting plain and dynamic text.
dir: ".lib/pages"
tags: [helper-text, hints, guidance, form, component, svelte, svelte-5, theui-svelte, component-library, ui, tailwindcss]
---

<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "$lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "$lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "$lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "$lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "$lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ConfigComponent from "$lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "$lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { HelperText, Input } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="Helper Text" text="The text under a form field: a description, a hint or an error message. It takes plain text or any markup you put inside it." edit_url={editURL("helper-text")} />

  <DocBlock title="About">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>HelperText</code> component shows a hint, an instruction or a validation message under a form input. Everything you place inside it becomes the helper text, so it can hold plain text or any elements and components.</p>
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Input</code>, <code>Select</code> and <code>FileInput</code> components use it internally through their <code>helperText</code> prop, which also links it to the input with <code>aria-describedby</code>. Use <code>HelperText</code> directly when you build a field yourself.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">To use the <b>HelperText</b> component, place the text inside it. Here's a basic example:</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <HelperText>Please enter your full name.</HelperText>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<script>
  import { HelperText } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<HelperText>Please enter your full name.</HelperText>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Helper Text Content">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">The content can be plain text or any mix of elements and components, which makes it flexible for hints, messages, or interactive content. A <code>HelperText</code> without content renders nothing.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
          <HelperText>Passwords must be at least 8 characters long.</HelperText>
          <HelperText><strong>Note:</strong>&nbsp;This field is required.</HelperText>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<!-- Plain text -->
<HelperText>Passwords must be at least 8 characters long.</HelperText>

<!-- With elements -->
<HelperText><strong>Note:</strong> This field is required.</HelperText>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Use With an Input">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">With <code>Input</code>, <code>Select</code> and <code>FileInput</code>, pass the helper text through the <code>helperText</code> prop instead of using the component directly. Those components render a <code>HelperText</code> for you, give it an <code>id</code> and link it to the input with <code>aria-describedby</code>, so screen readers read it together with the label.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <Input name="password" type="password" helperText="Must be at least 8 characters long">Password</Input>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Input name="password" type="password" helperText="Must be at least 8 characters long">Password</Input>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Customization">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">The <code>HelperText</code> component can be customized using the <code>class</code> attribute to apply additional styles or override default ones. Any other attributes you add, like <code>id</code> or <code>data-*</code>, are passed to the element.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <HelperText class="text-blue-600 italic">Please enter your full name.</HelperText>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<HelperText class="text-blue-600 italic">Please enter your full name.</HelperText>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <p class="not-prose"><code>HelperText</code> gives users additional guidance without distraction, and is styled to stay secondary in the visual hierarchy. When you use it through the <code>helperText</code> prop of an input, it is connected to that input with <code>aria-describedby</code> automatically. When you use the component directly, give it an <code>id</code> and add <code>aria-describedby</code> with that id to your input, so screen readers announce the two together.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <ConfigComponent {component} />
  </DocBlock>

  {#snippet sidebar()}
    <a href={processID("About")}>About</a>
    <a href={processID("Example")}>Example</a>
    <a href={processID("Helper Text Content")}>Helper Text Content</a>
    <a href={processID("Use With an Input")}>Use With an Input</a>
    <a href={processID("Customization")}>Customization</a>
    <a href={processID("Accessibility")}>Accessibility</a>
    <a href={processID("Configuration")}>Configuration</a>
  {/snippet}


</DocLayout>